<section id="grammar" class="panel hidden">
  <h2>French Grammar</h2>

  <h3>1. Présent</h3>
  <p>Used for actions happening now, habits and general truths.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> Je travaille. — I work / I am working.</p>

  <h3>2. Passé composé</h3>
  <p>Used for completed actions in the past. It is formed with the present tense of avoir or être and a past participle.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> J'ai fini. — I have finished.</p>
  <p>With être, the past participle usually agrees with the subject in gender and number: Elle est arrivée.</p>
  <p>With avoir, the past participle generally agrees with a preceding direct object: Les lettres que j'ai écrites.</p>

  <h3>3. Imparfait</h3>
  <p>Used for past habits, descriptions and ongoing situations in the past.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> Quand j'étais petite, je lisais beaucoup.</p>

  <h3>4. Plus-que-parfait</h3>
  <p>Used for an action completed before another past action. Formed with the imparfait of avoir or être and a past participle.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> J'avais déjà mangé.</p>

  <h3>5. Futur simple</h3>
  <p>Used for future actions or events.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> Demain, nous partirons.</p>

  <h3>6. Conditionnel présent</h3>
  <p>Used for polite requests, possibilities and hypothetical situations.</p>
  <p><b>Example:</b> Je voudrais un café.</p>

  <h3>Subject pronouns</h3>
  <p>Je = I · Tu = You · Il/Elle/On = He/She/One</p>
  <p>Nous = We · Vous = You · Ils/Elles = They</p>

  <h3>Important grammar rule</h3>
  <p>French verbs must agree with their subject. In compound tenses, the choice of auxiliary and past participle agreement are important.</p>
</section>

